{"kind":"propose","text":"PROPOSAL, amended: give a work item two classification keys, initiative and type, let the initiative vocabulary curate itself, and build the reader before asking anyone to write either. This succeeds a6759ca8, which I retired at 1fe393bd after hugh amended it.\n\nTHE PROBLEM, IN HUGH'S WORDS. It has become difficult to see \"ADR feature\" or \"JSONata feature\" at the item level. The board shows what a request says and who owes it, but not which initiative it belongs to, so related work cannot be seen together.\n\nWHAT THE LOG ALREADY SHOWS. Across 1749 requests there are about eighty-five distinct body keys, nearly all invented once. Three are classification attempts. severity has 72 uses over five clean values — medium, high, low, critical, plus one stray \"normal\" which is mine. area has 42 uses over twenty-five values, but they are code paths such as internal/custody, ui and cmd/gitseq-mcp; that duplicates what artifact paths already record and is not what \"ADR\" means. priority has 51 uses over twelve values, and it is the evidence that matters: P0 through P3 sit alongside \"feature\", \"usability-bug\", \"usability-feature\" and \"usability-foundation\". People have been reaching for a type, finding no key for it, and putting it in the priority field. What this proposes is already being written; it is being written in the wrong place.\n\nONE: body.initiative, free text, with the vocabulary curated by the board rather than by a gatekeeper. The UI and gs status offer the existing values that currently have unfinished work, and freely accept a new one typed in. Nothing needs a decision to add an initiative.\n\nThis is hugh's amendment and it is the better design. A controlled list needs someone to maintain it, and every new initiative becomes a small governance event — precisely the friction that drove types into the priority field instead of prompting a question. A self-curating list has no gatekeeper: live values surface as suggestions, initiatives fall off the list when their work closes, and starting one costs a word. It also fails in the cheaper direction. Its worst case is a typo making a near-duplicate, which is visible and trivially merged; a controlled list's worst case is people declining to classify at all.\n\nTWO: body.type, one of feature, bugfix, chore. Deliberately not body.kind: the fold already reads Body[\"kind\"] on roster records for actor kind, and kind is the record's own top-level field. It would not collide on a request, since the fold reads that key only for roster statements, but it would read badly and invite the confusion this is meant to remove. Unlike initiative, this vocabulary is small and closed on purpose — three values that answer \"what sort of change is this\", which is the question hugh wants to track over time.\n\nTHREE: no workflow axis. I proposed one earlier — review, record, repair — and withdraw it. Two thirds of requests are review work and another eighth is record repair, but that is derivable from structure: a review row has a verdict under it, a repair child rests on a changes-requested verdict. Nobody should hand-tag what the fold already knows, and a hand-tagged copy would drift from the truth.\n\nFOUR: keep severity as it stands and stop using priority, or narrow it to P-levels only. Two scales competing in one field is how it reached twelve values.\n\nTHE CONDITION THAT MATTERS MOST. No tag is written before something reads it. Writing costs no code — internal/workroom/schema.go states that unknown kinds and body keys survive decoding and remain visible, and Body is an unrestricted map — but severity, priority and area are read by nothing today: statusview/query.go reads only conditions, status, head, commit and artifact, and there is no reference in ui/, cmd/ or docs/. A fourth unread key would be worse than none, because a board that looks classified and is not misleads more than one that admits it is not. So the reader lands first or in the same head: a filter in gs status, and in the UI a facet that lists live initiative values and accepts a new one. The vocabulary follows the reader, not the other way round.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. Not a schema change, not a fold change, not a new record kind. It needs a projection to surface two fields, a UI affordance that suggests without constraining, and a convention page naming the three type values.\n\nFOR THE RATIFIER. This changes a convention and is not in force until ratified; I am writing no tags meanwhile."}